      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "PRREV-SCOPE-001",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Carried forward, updated for the new head 92aac9b4e3: this run reads IN FULL the 1-commit delta since 54b99dfdc7 (92aac9b4e3 fix(guard): C13 read a present release asset as MISSING) -- 2 files changed outside evidence/pr-review (scripts/check_release_assets.sh, scripts/release_criteria.sh), 6 insertions/3 deletions. Everything the prior receipt (7218961ef9) already sampled or carried forward retains that disposition and is NOT re-read this run except where explicitly re-checked below."
          },
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "measured",
            "command": [
              "git",
              "diff",
              "--stat",
              "54b99dfdc7",
              "92aac9b4e329fdf6c7907602761aeaa9d4186330",
              "--",
              ".",
              ":!evidence/pr-review"
            ],
            "exit_code": 0,
            "failure_scenario": "A reader could assume this receipt re-reviewed the full PR; it reviewed the 1-commit/2-file delta plus everything the prior receipts already sampled, not the whole diff again.",
            "precision_class": "advisory"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "PRREV-ASSETGUARD-001",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "RESOLVED this run: 92aac9b4e3 fixes scripts/check_release_assets.sh:113 (check_tag's per-asset membership test) and scripts/release_criteria.sh:85 (the C0 leg-1 '✗' check) -- both were `printf ... | grep -q ...` under the file's own `set -uo pipefail`. grep -q exits on its first match; the still-writing printf/upstream side of the pipe then takes SIGPIPE (rc 141), pipefail propagates that as the pipeline's exit status, and the `if` reads a PRESENT asset (or a matched '✗') as absent. Both are replaced with a here-string (`grep -qxF -- \"$want\" <<< \"$have\"` / `grep -q '✗' <<< \"$out\"`), which removes the pipeline and the SIGPIPE race entirely. Independently reproduced the defect on the OLD code and the fix on the NEW code: built a 20016-line asset list (the 16 real names + `seq -f 'extra-asset-%06g.tar.gz' 1 20000`, exceeding the 64 KiB pipe buffer so the race is deterministic) and ran `check_tag` against it 3x each -- old script rc=1 (false MISSING) all 3 runs, new script rc=0 (correctly credited) all 3 runs. Also independently ran `bash scripts/check_release_assets.sh --selftest` (12/12, including new row 6, the 20000-extra-asset regression row) and `bash scripts/release_criteria.sh --self-test` (10/10, including row 6, C13 credited on a complete release) against the live tree: both green. Also checked the empty-$have edge case by hand (`grep -qxF -- \"foo\" <<< \"\"`): correctly reports no match / MISSING, so the here-string does not introduce a new false-positive path for an empty release. No defect found in this delta; the fix is correct and does not weaken either guard -- a genuinely MISSING asset or a genuinely unmatched '✗' still reports as such (verified via existing selftest rows 2-5, 7 and C13's row 7)."
          },
          "properties": {
            "grounding": "measured",
            "command": [
              "bash",
              "scripts/check_release_assets.sh",
              "--selftest"
            ],
            "exit_code": 0,
            "failure_scenario": "Without this fix, any release asset list large enough to fill the pipe buffer before grep's first match (or any run that loses the race under load) reads a complete release as missing its apr binaries -- the exact CI red this delta closes (run 36510076745, guard-tree step 26, release_criteria.sh --self-test row 6).",
            "precision_class": "advisory"
          }
        },
        {
